WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.293
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.293 --> 00:00:08.650
I want you to imagine a very dangerous
day.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:14.310
You wake up in the morning looking like
this after a very, very peaceful night.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:18.640
However, the house you've just woken up in
is in an area that's surrounded

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.260
by a place which is very prone to
earthquakes, tornadoes and hurricanes.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.550
You get out of bed, you go down to your
bathroom.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.820
You fill up your bathroom sink full of
water and

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.710
you push aside all of the electrical
appliances next to you.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.697
You don't get electricuted on the way out
of the bathroom, on the way out of

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:39.686
the house you grab the only thing you can
find for breakfast which happens to

00:00:39.686 --> 00:00:44.560
be a very high in fat, high in calorie,
high sugar, high cholesterol breakfast.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.360
That's the only thing you have.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.630
On the way out of the house you narrowly
avoid your swimming pool,

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.850
you don't fall into your swimming pool,
and you do not drown.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:57.075
And as you get to your car, you narrowly
avoid eye contact with your neighbor.

00:00:57.075 --> 00:00:59.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Whom you know really hates you and

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.815
has an assault rifle in his back yard.

00:01:01.815 --> 00:01:03.590
[LAUGH].

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.200
You get into your car, you start driving,
you turn on the radio,

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.360
you hear stories of ISIS, Ebola, measles,
all of these risks,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.010
some of the most contagious and deadly
diseases to ever reach American soil.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.020
You get to your destination, which happens
to be an airport.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.730
You get into a giant tube of metal, you go
up 35,000 feet in the air,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:29.930
supported only by wings which are narrower
narrow and thinner than your car door.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.500
You're fine, and just before you go to
sleep,

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.250
you are reaching to the seat pocket in
front of you, you grab the bag of peanuts,

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.020
you read the choking hazard, you're fine.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.200
You eat the peanuts, you go to sleep,
everything's okay.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.210
Pretty dangerous day.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.280
Did you know that out of all of these
risks that you had today, the peanuts,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.504
the angry neighbor with the assault rifle,
the high calorie breakfast, the swimming

00:01:54.504 --> 00:01:58.040
pool, Ebola, none of these were the most
dangerous things you did today.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.438
It was actually getting into your car and
driving.

00:02:01.438 --> 00:02:07.059
Every year, 1.2 million people die on our
roads.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:14.110
To put that into perspective, that's the
equivalent of a jumbo jet full

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:19.564
of people falling out of the sky every
three hours.

00:02:19.564 --> 00:02:23.440
Every three hours, every single day,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.240
every single week, every single month,
every single year.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.160
Now I came to Stanford to think what can I
do?

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.810
What is my role in preventing the massive
spectrum of human suffering?

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.700
And what can I do to substantially improve
human life?

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.350
And when I look at these statistics,

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.080
I think this is a pretty good place to
start.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.310
Why does this happen?

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.270
What can we do about it?

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:57.430
Well it turns out that 93% of accidents
happen because of human error.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:58.080
Why?

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.095
Because we are human.

00:02:59.095 --> 00:03:00.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.180
&gt;&gt; We get careless.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.360
We make mistakes.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:07.420
We get distracted.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.190
We're irrational.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.120
We make bad decisions.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.480
We are human.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.920
The problem is though with accidents that
when we get into one,

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.750
we don't just affect ourselves but we
affect other people around us as well.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.920
Therefore if you want to do something to
substantially reduce the number of people

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.760
who die on our roads, we need to do
something about taking out.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.780
Some of the human elements from driving,
and replacing it by computers

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:37.150
that can make better, faster, more
accurate decisions on our behalf.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.580
And it looks like this, an autonomous
vehicle.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:47.140
Now as soon as I say, replacing some of
the human elements

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:51.720
of driving with cold hard computers, I'm
already feeling this visceral reaction

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.370
that we have in this room about extracting
from the warm, fuzzy,

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.150
emotional human elements and replacing it
with cold, hard numbers.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.010
But I want you to go with me for one
moment.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:03.640
Go with me for a second.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.080
I want you to imagine a safer world.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.080
I want you to imagine a safer world where
35,000 people

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.270
do not die on our roads every single year.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.130
That's two people since this talk began.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.685
I want you to imagine a safer world where
you do not have to share your roads

00:04:21.685 --> 00:04:26.630
with 65% of other drivers who are using
their cell phones at the same time.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:30.270
I want you to imagine a safer world where

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:34.980
the 112 million people who drive under the
influence of alcohol are not driving on

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.470
the same roads that my future children and
your children are going to be playing on.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.620
And lastly I want you to imagine a, a
safer world where you never have to look

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:50.300
for parking again, nor pay another parking
fine, imagine that world.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.320
Why aren't we there yet?

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.530
To me it comes down to how we make
decisions about adopting new technologies,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.660
both as consumers and also as regulators.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.730
You see good decision making about whether
to adopt new technology or

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:12.510
not, is made by understanding diligently
the benefits and the cost.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.080
If the benefits outweigh the cost,

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.910
then you do adopt the new technology,
otherwise you do not.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.630
The problem is that with a ton less
vehicles,

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:25.040
we have no idea what the benefits are,
because we haven't tried them.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:26.860
We haven't given them a shot.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:31.630
On the other hand, as soon as we start
thinking of the risks, given that we

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:36.900
live in such a risk-averse society, we
blow these risks out of proportion.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.693
And we, we engage our irrationalities and
fears to,

00:05:39.693 --> 00:05:42.000
to make them much larger than what they
are.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.821
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:45.821 --> 00:05:49.549
And that's something to think about
because when we want to understand which

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:53.300
technologies to adopt, we need to
understand both of them.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:06:00.440
Now, additionally to being at the GSB, I'm
also a magician and my, and my job as

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:06.120
a magician is to understand where the line
is drawn between perception and reality.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.530
And when I think of the risks of
autonomous vehicles,

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:13.470
I don't see reality, I see perceived
risks.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.430
I'll explain.
You see, a lot of people

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:22.390
think it's terrible to give up their
control of their driving to someone else,

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:26.050
but they have no issue about getting into
an Uber and

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.060
giving up their control of the driving to
someone they have no idea about.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.650
They have no issue and we have no issue
about getting into an airplane and

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:38.280
letting our flights be controlled by
computers on autopilot.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.730
We have no issue getting into a New York
subway or London underground and

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:46.410
having the, the flow of the carriages
controlled by co, co,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.130
cold hard numerical algorithms there.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:54.090
Computers already make life or death
decisions for us on a daily basis,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:59.990
and it's time to apply that to the number
one cause of death

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.740
for people between the ages of 3 and 34.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.630
So, what I ask of you is not to put aside
your

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.915
fears of autonomous vehicles but, to keep
them into proportion and

00:07:11.915 --> 00:07:16.025
also give necessary credence to their
benefits as well.

00:07:16.025 --> 00:07:20.745
The fact that we don't have to live in a
world where 35,000 people die

00:07:20.745 --> 00:07:23.082
on just US roads every year.

00:07:23.082 --> 00:07:25.602
The fact that you will not have to share

00:07:25.602 --> 00:07:29.872
your roads with people who are either
drunk or texting, the fact that you will

00:07:29.872 --> 00:07:34.792
have countless extra hours every year of
extra economic productivity.

00:07:35.992 --> 00:07:41.420
What I ask of you is not to just discount
autonomous vehicles as a new

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.860
in the thing, the new fad of Silicon
Valley.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:49.740
But to go out and to tell your friends, to
tell your colleagues, to tell your

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:54.649
family about how much safer this world
would be if we'd begin to adopt them.

00:07:54.649 --> 00:07:59.060
One day, I want to look back into the
world that we live in today,

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:04.047
where there was so much unnecessary human
suffering and think that we and

00:08:04.047 --> 00:08:07.501
autonomous vehicles were part of that
solution.

00:08:07.501 --> 00:08:08.245
Thank you.

00:08:08.245 --> 00:08:10.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:15.319
[MUSIC]

